}
\section{Columns of \code{records}}{
The first two columns say which input each row came from - \code{source_index} is 1-based in R, so it indexes \code{files} directly - and rows are grouped by input, in input order, whatever \code{threads} is.
\describe{
\item{\code{source_index}}{int; never \code{NA}. 0-based position of the row's library in the stdin list.}
\item{\code{source_path}}{string; never \code{NA}. Absolute path of the row's library.}
\item{\code{precursor_index}}{int; never \code{NA}. 0-based position of the precursor in the library (MslPrecursorIndexEntry.PrecursorIdx): the key MslLibrary.GetEntry takes, and the row order. Not m/z order.}
\item{\code{full_sequence}}{string; never \code{NA}. Modified sequence in mzLib bracket notation, e.g. PEPTM[Common Variable:Oxidation on M]IDE (MslLibraryEntry.FullSequence).}
\item{\code{base_sequence}}{string; never \code{NA}. The bare residues (MslLibraryEntry.BaseSequence).}
\item{\code{precursor_mz}}{float; in \strong{m/z}; never \code{NA}. Precursor m/z, (M + zH)/z (MslLibraryEntry.PrecursorMz), stored as a 32-bit float.}
\item{\code{charge_state}}{int; in \strong{charge}; never \code{NA}. Precursor charge (MslLibraryEntry.ChargeState).}
\item{\code{retention_time}}{float; in \strong{iRT or min (per row)}; never \code{NA}. Indexed retention time, or a run-calibrated time in minutes, as retention_time_unit says (MslLibraryEntry.RetentionTime). iRT can be negative. 0 is passed through: mzLib documents no sentinel for it.}
\item{\code{retention_time_unit}}{string; never \code{NA}. iRT or minutes, from MslLibraryEntry.RtIsCalibrated (false: iRT; true: run-specific minutes). Per row because one library can hold both.}
\item{\code{ion_mobility}}{float; in \strong{1/K0 (V s/cm2)}; \code{NA} when mzLib's 0.0, which MslLibraryEntry.IonMobility documents as not available; in absent_fields when no precursor has one. Ion mobility as 1/K0 (MslLibraryEntry.IonMobility).}
\item{\code{is_decoy}}{bool; never \code{NA}. A decoy precursor, entrapment decoys included (MslLibraryEntry.IsDecoy).}
\item{\code{is_entrapment}}{bool; never \code{NA}. An entrapment precursor, target or decoy (MslLibraryEntry.IsEntrapment, mzLib #1383). Independent of is_decoy.}
\item{\code{decoy_contaminant_target}}{string; never \code{NA}. T, D, ET or ED, from DecoyContaminantTargetLabel.For(is_decoy, false, is_entrapment). The .msl format has no contaminant flag, so C never appears.}
\item{\code{protein_accession}}{string; \code{NA} when mzLib's empty string: the precursor has no protein (MslLibraryEntry.ProteinAccession); in absent_fields when the library has no protein table. Accession of the source protein, as stored, e.g. P04637.}
\item{\code{protein_name}}{string; \code{NA} when mzLib's empty string: no protein, or no name for it (MslLibraryEntry.ProteinName); in absent_fields when the library has no protein table. Protein name, as stored.}
\item{\code{gene_name}}{string; \code{NA} when mzLib's empty string: no protein, or no gene for it (MslLibraryEntry.GeneName); in absent_fields when the library has no gene names. Gene symbol, as stored, e.g. TP53.}
\item{\code{is_proteotypic}}{bool; never \code{NA}. Expected to give a uniquely detectable signal (MslLibraryEntry.IsProteotypic), as the library's writer set it.}
\item{\code{q_value}}{float; in \strong{fraction (0 to 1)}; \code{NA} when mzLib's NaN: no q-value is available, as for a purely predicted library (MslLibraryEntry.QValue). The library's q-value for the precursor; lower is better.}
\item{\code{elution_group_id}}{int; never \code{NA}. Shared by every precursor of one base sequence, so charge states and modified forms co-elute (MslLibraryEntry.ElutionGroupId); mzLib's writer assigns it.}
\item{\code{molecule_type}}{string; never \code{NA}. MslFormat.MoleculeType name: Peptide, Proteoform, Oligonucleotide or Glycopeptide.}
\item{\code{source_type}}{string; never \code{NA}. MslFormat.SourceType name (MslLibraryEntry.Source): Predicted, Empirical, or EmpiricalRefined (predicted intensities, measured retention time or ion mobility).}
\item{\code{dissociation_type}}{string; never \code{NA}. mzLib DissociationType name, e.g. HCD; Unknown when the library does not say (MslLibraryEntry.DissociationType).}
\item{\code{nce}}{int; in \strong{NCE}; \code{NA} when mzLib's 0: unknown or not applicable (MslLibraryEntry.Nce). Normalized collision energy, a whole number: the format stores NCE x 10 and mzLib divides by 10 as an integer.}
\item{\code{fragment_count}}{int; in \strong{fragment ions}; never \code{NA}. Fragment ions the precursor has, counted from the ions mzLib read; 0 for a precursor with none, whose one row has every fragment cell null. \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{fragment_mz}}{float; in \strong{m/z}; \code{NA} when the precursor has no fragment ions (fragment_count 0). Fragment m/z (MslFragmentIon.Mz), a 32-bit float. Rows are in the stored order, which mzLib's writer sorts by m/z. \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{fragment_intensity}}{float; in \strong{relative intensity (0 to 1)}; \code{NA} when the precursor has no fragment ions. Intensity relative to the precursor's most intense fragment, which mzLib's writer scales to 1 (MslFragmentIon.Intensity). Not comparable across precursors. \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{product_type}}{string; \code{NA} when the precursor has no fragment ions. mzLib ProductType name, e.g. b, y, c, zDot; for an internal ion, its N-terminal type (MslFragmentIon.ProductType). \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{secondary_product_type}}{string; \code{NA} when a terminal ion (MslFragmentIon.SecondaryProductType is null for every terminal ion), or no fragment ions. An internal ion's C-terminal ProductType, e.g. y for bIy. \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{fragment_number}}{int; \code{NA} when the precursor has no fragment ions. The ion's number in its series, e.g. 5 for b5; for an internal ion, its 0-based start residue (MslFragmentIon.FragmentNumber). \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{secondary_fragment_number}}{int; \code{NA} when a terminal ion, for which mzLib stores 0 (MslFragmentIon.SecondaryFragmentNumber), or no fragment ions. An internal ion's end residue, exclusive. \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{residue_position}}{int; \code{NA} when mzLib's 0: always for a fragment with a custom neutral loss, whose stored position the format reuses to index its loss table (MslReader.ConvertFragment); or no fragment ions. Residue position the ion was annotated from, as stored (MslFragmentIon.ResiduePosition): for a library mzLib built from its own products, the Product.ResiduePosition convention, fragment_number for N-terminal ions and length - fragment_number + 1 for C-terminal ones (mzLib #1408). \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{fragment_charge}}{int; in \strong{charge}; \code{NA} when the precursor has no fragment ions. Fragment charge (MslFragmentIon.Charge). \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{neutral_loss}}{float; in \strong{Da}; \code{NA} when the precursor has no fragment ions. Neutral-loss mass, negative for a loss (MslFragmentIon.NeutralLoss). 0 means no loss, and crosses as 0, not null: it is the value of the shift, not a missing one. \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{is_internal}}{bool; \code{NA} when the precursor has no fragment ions. An internal ion (MslFragmentIon.IsInternalFragment). \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{is_diagnostic}}{bool; \code{NA} when the precursor has no fragment ions. A diagnostic ion, ProductType D (MslFragmentIon.IsDiagnosticIon). \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{exclude_from_quant}}{bool; \code{NA} when the precursor has no fragment ions. Marked to be left out of quantification, like DIA-NN's ExcludeFromAssay (MslFragmentIon.ExcludeFromQuant). \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
\item{\code{annotation}}{string; \code{NA} when the precursor has no fragment ions. mzLib's library rendering (MslFragmentIon.Annotation), e.g. b5, y3-18.0106, bIy[2-5] or y4^2+: the loss signed to four decimals and the charge appended, unlike Product.Annotation. \emph{Present only with \code{__fragments}.}}
}
}
\section{Errors}{
Each is an R condition carrying the class shown and \code{mzlib_error}; see \code{\link{mzlib_error}}.

\describe{
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{path missing or blank; the file does not exist, or is a directory; its extension is not .msl (MslFileTypeHandler.IsMslFile)}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{fragments given a value; index-only-threshold, offset, limit or out given with no value; index-only-threshold not a whole number, or below 0; offset or limit below 0 or not an integer; out resolving to the same file as path}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{out (with path or paths-stdin) whose extension is not .tsv, any case, including none (PYB-1); checked before any input is opened}
\item{\code{mzlib_bridge_error} (correctness)}{mzLib cannot read the library: FormatException for a bad magic number, version, footer or precursor count, InvalidDataException for a CRC-32 mismatch (MslReader); the error type is mzLib's exception name}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{paths-stdin with path, offset or limit; paths-stdin with a value; no paths on stdin; a repeated path; threads 0 or below -1; on-error not fail or skip; on-error skip with a single path; out naming an input}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{with on-error fail, an input that is missing or not a .msl: the usage error of that input, prefixed 'Input <i> (<path>)'}
\item{\code{mzlib_bridge_error} (correctness)}{with on-error fail, mzLib failing to read an input: that input's own error type, message prefixed 'Input <i> (<path>)'}
\item{\code{mzlib_usage_error} (usage)}{the answer is too large to return as one JSON document (PYB-3 part 1). Message: 'this read (N records) is too large to return as one document; use \code{--limit}/\code{--offset} or \code{--out}'. A library with fragments is the likeliest case: write it to out.}
\item{\code{mzlib_service_unavailable} (service_unavailable)}{Never raised by this verb.}
}
}
\section{Caveats}{
\itemize{
\item Rows are in the library's stored order, by precursor_index, not by m/z: mzLib's own MslLibrary.GetAllEntries enumerates in ascending precursor m/z instead. Sort on precursor_mz for that order.
\item retention_time is iRT for some precursors and run minutes for others: read retention_time_unit on each row (MslLibraryEntry.RtIsCalibrated). A library can hold both, so never plot or compare the column without it. There is no conversion: iRT becomes minutes only through a calibration against a run.
\item The library is unfiltered: decoys (D, ED) and entrapment precursors (ET, ED) are rows. Filter on is_decoy, or on decoy_contaminant_target, before counting targets.
\item A retention_time of 0 may mean the source had none: MslLibraryEntry.FromLibrarySpectrum, the path mzLib converts a text library through, writes RetentionTime ?? 0.0, and mzLib documents no sentinel for it, so 0 is passed through as a value.
\item nce is a whole number: the format stores NCE x 10 and MslReader reads it back with integer division, so an NCE of 27.5 reads as 27.
\item A library converted from a text library (.msp) has no NCE, dissociation type, q-value, ion mobility or protein: LibrarySpectrum has no field for them, and MslLibraryEntry.FromLibrarySpectrum fills mzLib's not-available defaults, so nce and q_value are null and dissociation_type is Unknown.
\item precursor_mz, retention_time and ion_mobility are stored as 32-bit floats and widened to double by mzLib, so 465.74 reads back as 465.739990234375. The extra digits are the float, not a measurement; round them for display.
\item offset and limit count PRECURSORS. With fragments, each precursor gives one row per fragment ion.
\item With fragments: residue_position is null for every fragment with a custom neutral loss, because the .msl format reuses that field to index its loss table and MslReader reads it back as 0.
\item With fragments: fragment_intensity is relative within its precursor, 0 to 1, because mzLib's writer scales each precursor's most intense fragment to 1 (MslWriter). It is not comparable across precursors.
\item Index-only (at or above index_only_threshold) keeps memory to the precursors, but with fragments every fragment block is read twice from disk: once to count the rows, once to write them. mzLib keeps each precursor's stored fragment count private, so the bridge cannot size the table without reading them.
}
}
\section{Performance}{
Every call starts one bridge process, which costs a .NET start-up before any work. This bulk form pays it once for the whole list, which is the reason it exists: never loop the one-path function over many files.
}
